        "id": "resolution-and-frame-rate-per-platform",
        "type": "prose",
        "heading": "Resolution and frame-rate per platform",
        "body": "| Platform | Performance mode | Quality mode | Other notes |\n|----------|------------------|--------------|-------------|\n| PS5 | 4K (dynamic ~1440p–4K via upscaling) at 60 FPS | Near-native 4K at 30–40 FPS, with a 40 FPS cap option for 120Hz displays | Performance mode can be uncapped to push 60–90 FPS on VRR displays. No in-game ray tracing toggle on base PS5. |\n| Xbox Series X | Mirrors PS5 exactly (4K / 60 Performance; near-4K / 30–40 Quality) | Same as PS5 | Same VRR uncapping behavior. |\n| PS5 Pro | PSSR2 upscaling (2304×1296 → 4K) at 60+ FPS | Exclusive ray-traced reflections toggle with no performance penalty | Capable of 90+ FPS uncapped with VRR. |\n| Xbox Series S | 1080p / 60 Performance | 1080p / 30–50 Quality | Lower base resolution than Series X. |\n| Steam (PC) | 1080p / 60 Recommended (medium settings) | 1080p / 30 Minimum (low settings) | DLSS 4.5 (NVIDIA) / FSR 3.1 (AMD) upscaling; frame-generation toggle supported. |\n| Switch 2 (TV / docked) | N/A | 1080p at 30 FPS, with an optional 30–40 FPS variable-rate option | Locked at 30 FPS in any mode. |\n| Switch 2 (handheld) | N/A | 900p at 30 FPS, with the same 30–40 FPS variable-rate option | Locked at 30 FPS in any mode. |\n\nPractical takeaway: if you want 4K / 60 from a console, pick PS5 or Xbox Series X. If you want a portable version that still plays at 30 FPS with reduced resolution, Switch 2 works but is the weakest of the platform modes. PC is the only platform that scales with your hardware and lets you choose DLSS 4.5 (NVIDIA) or FSR 3.1 (AMD) plus a frame-generation toggle."
